      },
      "widgets_values": [
        "subject_definitions:\n<Subject 1> is Mara, Part-time school technician, replacement application maintainer and sole on-camera vocalist., defined by <Picture 2>. Woman, approximately 34, with medium-brown skin, an angular face and chin-length straight black hair tucked behind one ear; compact silhouette; rust overshirt, charcoal T-shirt, black trousers, dark sneakers and narrow silver wristwatch. Owns the graphite laptop and external keyboard.\n<Picture 1> is the opening frame, supplying the actual setting, composition and pose.\n<Audio 1> is the isolated recorded vocal for <Subject 1> (S1), aligned at clip time zero. The original performance, including held notes, breaths and pauses, is preserved continuously.\n\nsummary:\n[keyframe completion + audio reference] One coherent music-video scene with the explicitly assigned cast.\n\nretention_analysis:\n<Subject 1> (appears in [Shot 1]): fully_preserved - exact identity, species, facial geometry, hair length, beard, markings, wardrobe and owned instrument from Picture 2.\n\n<Picture 1> ([Shot 1] first frame): fully_preserved - opening composition, location and pose.\n<Audio 1>: reference - recorded syllable timing and silent rests only.\n\ndetailed_description:\nUse the visual medium, era, lighting and cinematography specified by the storyboard. Preserve natural anatomy and coherent physical movement.\n[Shot 1] Mara rests one hand on her black working chair's arm.\nStoryboard direction: Slow push toward a tight frontal portrait, with the empty black collaborator chair at the edge.\nBegin with: Mara sits alone behind idle equipment.\nBy the final frame: The frame emphasizes her enclosed position.\nThe action serves this story purpose: Reflect Mara's isolation within the conflict.\nScene continuity: Use the distinct performance axis without advancing the application state. The oak checkout chair is elsewhere.\nEach character sheet shows multiple views of ONE identity, not multiple people. Render each assigned character exactly once. Do not reproduce the grid, reference background, alternate views or sheet layout. Keep every role attached to its own identity; never exchange the guitarist and bassist, hairstyles, facial hair, costumes or instruments. Only the listed scene cast is visible. \n <Subject 1> (S1) performs the actual recorded vocal in <Audio 1>. The continuous recording is the timing authority: match its audible syllables, held notes, breaths and natural pauses from clip time zero. Begin mouth articulation with the audible voice and let the lips settle during actual vocal rests. Follow the recorded pace throughout; do not invent, repeat, anticipate, omit or retime words. Keep the lips visible and the face unobscured while the body carries out the scene action.\nThe continuous recorded vocal takes priority over estimated word timings, scene notes and source mouth motion. Instrumental performance means hands and body playing the assigned instrument.\nKeep each face recognizable as its own character at every camera distance, angle and motion speed. Preserve hair length, beard shape, body silhouette and garment structure from first frame to last.\nThe scene is one continuous physical moment. Keep all prop counts and ownership consistent, with hands attached naturally to the correct body and instrument.\nClothing moves with the body while retaining its color and shape. Light direction and shadows remain consistent with the environment. Background architecture stays coherent as the camera moves.\nUse one camera setup and one simple action, with no internal cuts, split screens, montage, scene change or new arrivals.\nNo sheet grid, duplicated character views, captions or overlays. The ending continues the same cast, physical action and setting across the scene boundary.\n\noverall_soundscape:\nN/A\n\nnon_diegetic_music:\nN/A"
